#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""GhostSpiral Telegram pager — the phone-to-Pi trigger, and nothing more.

OPSEC_SETUP.md §5 steps 1–2 have always been procedure with no tool behind
them: "Phone: /recv then /depo 0.05 to the throwaway account. Pi checks Tor,
allowlisted chat id, rate limit." This is that tool. Step 3 onward is
unchanged — it pokes `gs_doorbell`, which does what it already did.

WHAT THIS IS ALLOWED TO DO, taken from the design it has to fit rather than
invented here:

  * §8: "Do not 'just run a Telegram bot' that prints the memo — that throws
    away the only reason to have a Pi." By DEFAULT this sends no address, no
    memo, no slip and no amount: the most it says is which job finished and a
    4-hex handle, exactly as §5 step 5 describes — "Telegram gets `depo ready
    · slip A3F1`".

    WHAT THIS PARAGRAPH USED TO CLAIM WITHOUT QUALIFICATION, and no longer
    can: that nothing readable is EVER sent here. The vault's own keyfile can
    now say otherwise, because "read it on the vault" is not an OPSEC property
    for an operator who cannot reach the vault — it is a quoted swap they have
    no way to pay. Three modes, and this box picks NONE of them:

        neither field set     a handle. What §8 describes, and the default.
        delivery_public       a sealed blob only gs_unseal can open.
        plain_slip            the deposit address and memo, in the clear.

    The choice lives in the VAULT's keyfile and nowhere else, so a stolen bot
    token or a compromised Pi cannot ask for plaintext — changing it means
    standing at the vault. What plaintext costs, including the part that is
    about money rather than privacy, is argued in gs_wake_proto's "THE
    PLAINTEXT SLIP". Read it before turning it on.
  * §8: "The only acceptable remote trigger is inbound over Tor to a Pi onion
    service: no port forward, and no WAN path to WOL." Long-polling satisfies
    that constraint more simply than an onion service does: the Pi makes an
    OUTBOUND connection to Telegram and listens on nothing at all. There is no
    inbound port to forward, so there is no WAN path to WOL either.
  * §4: "The bot (when it exists) talks to Telegram ONLY via
    socks5h://127.0.0.1:9050. If Tor is down, the bot does not start. Same
    fail-closed as gs_console." Enforced at startup and on every request:
    safe_get/safe_post abort on a falsy proxies dict, which is why they are
    used here instead of requests directly.
  * §8: the wake channel takes three jobs and no others, "every parameter is a
    bounded integer or a 4-hex handle", and "there is deliberately no job that
    takes an XMR destination". This composes params from the operator's digits
    only; the job schema in gs_wake_proto validates them again.

WHY /depo TAKES A SLOT AND NOT AN AMOUNT. §8: "the amount is an INDEX into a
ladder that lives on the ThinkPad, so the Pi never sends a number". The Pi's
own keyfile does not contain the ladder — checked: it holds secret,
peer_public, target_mac, wol_broadcast, wol_port, listen_host, listen_port and
nothing else — so the Pi genuinely cannot turn "0.05" into a slot, and a
pager that accepted an amount would have to either carry the ladder (putting
it on the SD card) or send a number (breaking the rule). `/depo 2` it is.

WHAT A STOLEN PHONE OR BOT TOKEN GETS, stated plainly because §7 already
scores it: they can wake the vault and spam quotes. They cannot spend, cannot
name a destination, and cannot read a memo — the reply vocabulary here has no
word for one. The real bound is on the ThinkPad and needs physical access to
change: a 24 h wake budget and an account ceiling in the keyfile. The rate
limit below is courtesy, not the backstop, and says so.

NOT A DAEMON THAT HOLDS SECRETS. The keyfile passphrase is read once at
startup from GS_WAKE_PASSPHRASE (gs_doorbell.read_passphrase's own documented
path for a headless trigger) and the bot token from GS_TG_BOT_TOKEN or a 0400
file. Neither is ever an argument: /proc/<pid>/cmdline is 0444 and every local
account can read it, which is the same reason the wallet password and the BTC
entry address were moved off command lines elsewhere in this repo.
"""
from __future__ import annotations
import argparse
import json
import os
import re
import sys
import threading
import time
from pathlib import Path

sys.path.insert(0, str(Path(__file__).resolve().parent))

from gs_common import (                                      # noqa: E402
    integrity_log, install_signal_handlers, shutdown_requested,
    isolated_proxy, safe_get, safe_post, validate_proxy, verify_tor,
    atomic_write_json, terminal_safe,
)
import gs_wake_proto as proto                                # noqa: E402

#: The token env var. Never a flag: see the module docstring.
TOKEN_ENV = "GS_TG_BOT_TOKEN"

#: Telegram holds the request open this long when there is nothing to say.
#: Deliberately under gs_common.safe_get's own 20 s client timeout, so the
#: audited helper can be reused rather than hand-rolling a request that would
#: also have to re-implement its fail-closed proxy check.
POLL_HOLD_S = 10

#: FAIL-CLOSED IS NOT ONLY A STARTUP PROPERTY.
#:
#: §4 says "If Tor is down, the bot does not start" -- and the first version
#: enforced that at startup and then, if Tor died an hour later, sat in a
#: 5-second retry loop forever. That is fail-OPEN in the only way that
#: matters: the operator believes a poke will work, and the bot has quietly
#: been unable to reach anything since. Consecutive failures are counted and
#: the process exits, so systemd's Restart= re-runs the startup verify_tor and
#: the bot stays down until Tor is genuinely back.
#:
#: Not 1: a single blip on a Tor circuit is ordinary and must not stop a
#: pager. At 5 s a retry this is about a minute of sustained failure.
MAX_POLL_FAILURES = 12

#: Seconds before the ONE retry of a sealed slip that did not send. Long enough
#: for a Tor circuit to be rebuilt, short enough that it does not hold `busy`
#: -- and therefore the next poke -- for anything an operator would notice.
SLIP_RETRY_S = 20

#: Courtesy limits. The REAL bound is the vault's 24 h wake budget, which lives
#: in a keyfile that needs physical access to change; this only stops a stolen
#: phone from queueing pokes faster than a human would.
DEFAULT_MIN_INTERVAL_S = 300
DEFAULT_DAILY_CAP = 12

#: A handle as gs_wake_proto defines it, anchored with \Z rather than $ because
#: `$` also matches before a trailing newline (see gs_common's validators).
HANDLE_RE = re.compile(r"[0-9A-Fa-f]{4}\Z")

HELP = (
    "GhostSpiral pager\n"
    "/recv [n]   mint n receive subaddresses (1-4, default 1)\n"
    "/depo <s>   quote a swap to ladder slot s (0-7)\n"
    "/check <h>  has my payment arrived? (~5 min, then it powers off)\n"
    "/watch <h>  wait up to 110 min for it (blocks everything else)\n"
    "/status     counters only\n"
    "\n"
    "WHAT COMES BACK depends on the VAULT's keyfile, not on this box:\n"
    "nothing readable, a SEALED slip for gs_unseal, or the deposit\n"
    "address and memo in the clear.\n"
    "\n"
    "The memo needs an OP_RETURN. No phone wallet can do that —\n"
    "use Electrum or Sparrow on a desktop. Any desktop; not the vault."
)


#: gs_doorbell, loaded ONCE, BY PATH.
#:
#: Python cannot `import gs_doorbell`: the shipped tools are extensionless on
#: purpose -- they are commands, not modules -- so the import system will not
#: find one by name. Driving this end to end is how that turned up; tests/ load
#: them the same way. Cached because two loads would mean two module objects
#: with two copies of its state.
_DOORBELL = [None]


def doorbell():
    import importlib.machinery
    import importlib.util
    if _DOORBELL[0] is None:
        path = str(Path(__file__).resolve().parent / "gs_doorbell")
        ld = importlib.machinery.SourceFileLoader("gs_doorbell", path)
        spec = importlib.util.spec_from_loader(ld.name, ld)
        mod = importlib.util.module_from_spec(spec)
        sys.modules.setdefault("gs_doorbell", mod)
        ld.exec_module(mod)
        _DOORBELL[0] = mod
    return _DOORBELL[0]


#: Every character a terminal treats as an instruction rather than as text.
#: C0, DEL and C1 -- the last of these matters because a lone 0x9b IS a CSI
#: introducer on a terminal in 8-bit mode, so stripping only ESC would leave a
#: working escape sequence behind.
_CTRL_RE = re.compile(r"[\x00-\x1f\x7f-\x9f]")


def _no_ctrl(text: str) -> str:
    """Remove control characters. NOT what terminal_safe does.

    terminal_safe redacts IDENTIFIERS -- addresses, MACs, bech32, paths -- and
    passes control characters through untouched, which is correct for its job
    and not enough for this one. A Telegram username is a string its owner
    chose, and anyone can message a bot they find; --whoami prints one to a
    terminal and, under systemd, to a journal. Measured before this existed:
    terminal_safe("a\\x1b[31mb\\x07c") returns it unchanged, escape and bell
    intact. gs_unseal hit the same gap from the other direction and uses
    instruction_field_safe to REFUSE; here the value is cosmetic, so it is
    stripped and the chat id still gets printed.
    """
    return _CTRL_RE.sub("", str(text))


def _redact(text: str) -> str:
    """Strip anything token-shaped out of a string before it is printed.

    Telegram puts the bot token in the URL PATH -- there is no header form --
    so the token is inside every request URL and therefore inside the text of
    any exception requests raises about one. This runs over everything that
    reaches a terminal or the journal.
    """
    out = re.sub(r"\bbot\d{6,}:[A-Za-z0-9_-]{20,}", "bot<token>", str(text))
    out = re.sub(r"\b\d{6,}:[A-Za-z0-9_-]{20,}", "<token>", out)
    # An exception's text is not always this program's words: a server can
    # shape a chunk of it, and requests puts response detail into some of
    # them. Same one-line rule as the username, for the same reason.
    return _no_ctrl(terminal_safe(out))


def load_token(token_file: str = "") -> str:
    """The bot token, from the environment or a 0400 file. NEVER from argv."""
    v = os.environ.get(TOKEN_ENV, "")
    if v:
        return v.strip()
    if token_file:
        p = Path(token_file)
        try:
            st = p.stat()
        except OSError as e:
            sys.exit(f"[!] --token-file {p}: {e.strerror}")
        if st.st_mode & 0o077:
            sys.exit(
                f"[!] {p} is mode {oct(st.st_mode & 0o777)}. A bot token that "
                f"other local accounts can read is a token they can use to "
                f"wake your vault. chmod 400 it.")
        return p.read_text(encoding="utf-8").strip()
    sys.exit(
        f"[!] No bot token. Set {TOKEN_ENV} in this process's environment, or "
        f"pass --token-file pointing at a 0400 file.\n"
        f"    NOT on the command line: /proc/<pid>/cmdline is world-readable, "
        f"so a token there is readable by every local account.")


class Limits:
    """Rate state that survives a restart, so a crash loop is not a bypass."""

    def __init__(self, path: Path, min_interval: int, daily_cap: int):
        self.path = path
        self.min_interval = min_interval
        self.daily_cap = daily_cap
        self.offset = 0            # Telegram update_id cursor
        self.last_poke = 0.0
        self.pokes = []            # wall-clock stamps, pruned to 24 h
        self._load()

    def _load(self) -> None:
        try:
            d = json.loads(self.path.read_text(encoding="utf-8"))
        except (OSError, ValueError):
            return
        if not isinstance(d, dict):
            return
        self.offset = int(d.get("offset") or 0)
        self.last_poke = float(d.get("last_poke") or 0)
        self.pokes = [float(x) for x in (d.get("pokes") or [])
                      if isinstance(x, (int, float))]

    def save(self) -> None:
        atomic_write_json(
            {"offset": self.offset, "last_poke": self.last_poke,
             "pokes": self.pokes[-200:]}, self.path, perms=0o600)

    def recent(self) -> list:
        now = time.time()
        self.pokes = [t for t in self.pokes if now - t < 86400]
        return self.pokes

    def why_not(self) -> str:
        """'' if a poke is allowed, else the reason to send back."""
        now = time.time()
        gap = now - self.last_poke
        if gap < self.min_interval:
            return (f"rate limited, {int(self.min_interval - gap)}s left "
                    f"(courtesy limit; the vault's own 24h budget is the "
                    f"real one)")
        if len(self.recent()) >= self.daily_cap:
            return (f"{self.daily_cap} pokes already in the last 24h "
                    f"(courtesy limit)")
        return ""

    def record(self) -> None:
        now = time.time()
        self.last_poke = now
        self.pokes.append(now)
        self.save()


def parse_command(text: str) -> tuple:
    """(job, params, error). Digits only -- never a destination, never a flag.

    Telegram message text is attacker-controlled the moment the phone or the
    token is: this reads a fixed vocabulary and a bounded integer and refuses
    everything else, rather than passing anything through to a command line.
    Nothing here can name an XMR address, because no job accepts one.
    """
    raw = (text or "").strip()
    # A command may arrive as "/depo@mybot 2" in a group.
    parts = raw.split()
    if not parts:
        return "", {}, "empty"
    cmd = parts[0].split("@", 1)[0].lower()
    arg = parts[1] if len(parts) > 1 else ""
    if len(parts) > 2:
        return "", {}, "too many arguments"

    if cmd in ("/help", "/start"):
        return "", {}, "help"
    if cmd == "/status":
        return "", {}, "status"
    if cmd == "/recv":
        n = 1
        if arg:
            if not arg.isdigit():
                return "", {}, "count must be a number 1-4"
            n = int(arg)
        if not 1 <= n <= 4:
            return "", {}, "count must be 1-4"
        return "receive_new", {"count": n}, ""
    if cmd == "/depo":
        if not arg.isdigit():
            return "", {}, "give a ladder SLOT 0-7, not an amount"
        s = int(arg)
        if not 0 <= s <= 7:
            return "", {}, "slot must be 0-7"
        return "receive_and_quote", {"amount_slot": s}, ""
    if cmd == "/watch":
        if not HANDLE_RE.match(arg):
            return "", {}, "handle must be 4 hex characters"
        return "watch", {"handle": arg.upper()}, ""
    if cmd == "/check":
        if not HANDLE_RE.match(arg):
            return "", {}, "handle must be 4 hex characters"
        return "swap_status", {"handle": arg.upper()}, ""
    return "", {}, "unknown command"


class Pager:
    def __init__(self, args, token: str, key: dict, proxies: dict):
        self.args = args
        self.token = token
        self.key = key
        self.proxies = proxies
        self.limits = Limits(Path(args.state), args.min_interval,
                             args.daily_cap)
        self.allow = set(int(c) for c in args.chat_id)
        self.busy = threading.Lock()
        self.ignored = 0
        self.poll_failures = 0

    # -- telegram -------------------------------------------------------
    def _url(self, method: str) -> str:
        return f"https://api.telegram.org/bot{self.token}/{method}"

    def send(self, chat_id: int, text: str) -> bool:
        """One reply. Never carries a plaintext address, memo or amount.

        RETURNS WHETHER IT LANDED, and that is not decoration. This used to
        return None and swallow the failure into a print() -- which under the
        shipped unit goes to StandardOutput=null, i.e. nowhere. That was
        survivable while every reply was a status line the operator could
        infer from the next one. It stopped being survivable when a reply
        started carrying the sealed slip: a dropped send left the operator
        looking at "Sealed slip below" with nothing below it, on a box they
        cannot walk to, for a quote that expires while they wait.
        """
        try:
            safe_post(self._url("sendMessage"),
                      {"chat_id": chat_id, "text": text,
                       "disable_web_page_preview": True},
                      proxies=self.proxies)
            return True
        except Exception as e:                               # noqa: BLE001
            print(f"  [!] reply failed: {_redact(e)}")
            return False

    def updates(self) -> list:
        url = (f"{self._url('getUpdates')}?timeout={POLL_HOLD_S}"
               f"&offset={self.limits.offset}&allowed_updates=%5B%22message%22%5D")
        try:
            d = safe_get(url, proxies=self.proxies)
        except Exception as e:                               # noqa: BLE001
            self.poll_failures += 1
            print(f"  [!] poll failed ({self.poll_failures}/"
                  f"{MAX_POLL_FAILURES}): {_redact(e)}")
            if self.poll_failures >= MAX_POLL_FAILURES:
                integrity_log("pager", "poll_failed_giving_up")
                sys.exit(
                    f"[!] {MAX_POLL_FAILURES} consecutive poll failures. "
                    f"Tor or the network is down.\n"
                    f"    Stopping rather than looping: a pager that cannot "
                    f"reach Telegram but keeps\n"
                    f"    running is one you believe is listening when it is "
                    f"not. It does not start\n"
                    f"    without Tor (section 4); it should not keep running "
                    f"without it either.")
            time.sleep(5)
            return []
        self.poll_failures = 0
        if not isinstance(d, dict) or not d.get("ok"):
            return []
        out = d.get("result")
        return out if isinstance(out, list) else []

    # -- the poke -------------------------------------------------------
    def poke(self, chat_id: int, job: str, params: dict) -> None:
        """Run one wake, in this process, and report only its OUTCOME.

        gs_doorbell is imported rather than shelled out to: its human-facing
        stdout is not an interface (this repo says so about JoinMarket's, and
        about parsing a tool's output to identify a bundle), and going through
        the module means the outcome and handle are read as values instead of
        scraped out of prose.
        """
        db = doorbell()
        try:
            pending = db.run_wake(self.args, self.key, job, params)
        except BaseException as e:                           # noqa: BLE001
            integrity_log("pager", f"wake_failed:{job}")
            self.send(chat_id, f"{job}: failed to start — {_redact(e)[:160]}")
            return
        out = pending.outcome()
        integrity_log("pager", f"outcome:{job}:{out}")
        if out == "done":
            res = pending.result or {}
            h = res.get("handle") or "----"
            slip = res.get("slip") or ""
            plain = res.get("plain") or {}
            phase = res.get("phase") or ""
            if phase:
                # A STATUS PROBE ANSWERS IN ONE SENTENCE and says nothing else.
                # The sentence comes from the protocol's own table so this box
                # cannot invent a reassurance, and the word it renders was
                # checked against a closed set by the doorbell before it got
                # here.
                self.send(chat_id, f"{h}: {proto.PHASE_LINES.get(phase, phase)}")
                return
            if plain:
                # THE DEPOSIT INSTRUCTIONS, IN THE CLEAR, because the operator's
                # own vault keyfile asked for it. gs_wake_proto's "THE PLAINTEXT
                # SLIP" is where that trade is argued; this branch only renders
                # it. Two messages: the memo must be copied alone (see below).
                lines = proto.plain_lines(plain)
                body, memo = lines[:-1], lines[-1]
                self.send(chat_id, f"{job} ready · slip {h}\n\n"
                                   + "\n".join(body).rstrip())
                # THE MEMO ALONE IN ITS OWN MESSAGE. Tap-and-hold copies a
                # WHOLE Telegram message, and this string has to reach a wallet
                # character-for-character or ThorChain cannot route the swap
                # and the money is stranded. Anything sharing the message would
                # be pasted with it.
                if not self.send(chat_id, memo):
                    time.sleep(SLIP_RETRY_S)
                    if not self.send(chat_id, memo):
                        integrity_log("pager", "memo_undelivered")
                        self.send(chat_id,
                                  f"The MEMO did not get through, twice. Do "
                                  f"NOT send the BTC without it — it would be "
                                  f"unroutable. Poke /depo again.")
                return
            if not slip:
                # NO DELIVERY KEY IS CONFIGURED, so there is nothing to send and
                # this is still the original vocabulary: §5 step 5, "Telegram
                # gets `depo ready · slip A3F1`". No address, no memo, no
                # amount.
                self.send(chat_id, f"{job} ready · slip {h}\n"
                                   f"Read the address and memo on the vault.")
                return
            # A SEALED SLIP CAME BACK. This process cannot read it: it is
            # sealed to the delivery key, which exists on neither this box nor
            # this SD card. See gs_wake_proto's "THE SEALED SLIP" for why that
            # is not the thing §8 forbids.
            #
            # THE BLOB GOES FIRST, AND THE CAPTION DESCRIBES WHAT ACTUALLY
            # HAPPENED. Sending the caption first meant promising a slip
            # before knowing one could be delivered: a dropped second send
            # left "Sealed slip below" with nothing below it, and the only
            # record of the failure was a print() that the shipped unit sends
            # to StandardOutput=null. Now the caption is written after the
            # fact and tells the truth either way.
            #
            # The blob is sent ALONE, with nothing else in the message, and
            # that is a usability decision with a security consequence:
            # tap-and-hold on a Telegram message copies the WHOLE message, so
            # a caption sharing the message would be pasted into gs_unseal
            # along with the slip. open_slip's length check would catch it,
            # but being caught is worse than not happening.
            # ONE RETRY BEFORE GIVING UP. The blob is already in hand and a
            # Tor circuit failing for one request is ordinary; telling the
            # operator to go to the vault because a single POST lost a race is
            # advice this whole feature exists to avoid having to give. One,
            # not a loop: a wake holds `busy` while this runs, and a retry
            # storm against a dead circuit would hold it for the rate limit's
            # entire window.
            ok = self.send(chat_id, slip)
            if not ok:
                time.sleep(SLIP_RETRY_S)
                ok = self.send(chat_id, slip)
            if ok:
                self.send(chat_id, f"{job} ready · slip {h}\n"
                                   f"↑ sealed slip. gs_unseal it on the "
                                   f"machine you send the BTC from.")
            else:
                integrity_log("pager", "slip_undelivered")
                # NOT "/watch {h}". That was the first version of this line and
                # it is wrong: watch waits for a payment to LAND on a bundle,
                # so it cannot hand back a deposit address the operator never
                # received -- it would sit for two hours waiting for money that
                # was never sent. The quote is unpayable from here, and a
                # ThorChain quote expires anyway, so the honest instruction is
                # to ask for a new one.
                self.send(chat_id,
                          f"{job} ready · slip {h}\n"
                          f"The sealed slip did NOT get through, twice. The "
                          f"job ran and the quote is on the vault — but it is "
                          f"unpayable from here, and quotes expire. Poke "
                          f"/depo again for a fresh one.")
        elif out == "refused":
            self.send(chat_id, f"{job}: vault REFUSED. Reason is on the vault.")
        elif out == "failed":
            self.send(chat_id, f"{job}: vault ran it and it FAILED. "
                               f"Reason is on the vault.")
        elif out == "expired_uncollected":
            self.send(chat_id, f"{job}: nothing collected it. Vault may be "
                               f"off or without Tor. Nothing was handed over.")
        else:
            self.send(chat_id, f"{job}: collected but never reported back. "
                               f"CHECK THE VAULT before poking again.")

    def _worker(self, chat_id: int, job: str, params: dict) -> None:
        try:
            self.poke(chat_id, job, params)
        finally:
            self.busy.release()

    # -- loop -----------------------------------------------------------
    def handle(self, upd: dict) -> None:
        msg = upd.get("message")
        if not isinstance(msg, dict):
            return
        chat = msg.get("chat") or {}
        cid = chat.get("id")
        if not isinstance(cid, int) or cid not in self.allow:
            # NOT replied to. Answering an unknown chat confirms the bot is
            # alive to whoever found it, and the token is the only thing
            # standing between a stranger and this loop.
            self.ignored += 1
            integrity_log("pager", "chat_not_allowlisted")
            return
        # A PHOTO IS NOT A COMMAND. Without this, every sticker, image or
        # voice note in an allowlisted chat drew the whole help text back --
        # noise in the one channel that is supposed to be almost silent, and
        # a reply to something nobody typed.
        if not isinstance(msg.get("text"), str):
            return
        job, params, err = parse_command(msg["text"])
        if err == "help":
            self.send(cid, HELP)
            return
        if err == "status":
            self.send(cid, f"pokes in last 24h: {len(self.limits.recent())}/"
                           f"{self.limits.daily_cap}\n"
                           f"ignored messages: {self.ignored}\n"
                           f"busy: {self.busy.locked()}")
            return
        if err:
            self.send(cid, f"no: {err}\n\n{HELP}")
            return
        why = self.limits.why_not()
        if why:
            self.send(cid, f"no: {why}")
            return
        # ONE AT A TIME. gs_doorbell binds a fixed LAN port for the window, so
        # a second concurrent wake cannot bind and would fail confusingly --
        # and two vaults woken at once is not a thing this design has.
        if not self.busy.acquire(blocking=False):
            self.send(cid, "no: a wake is already running")
            return
        self.limits.record()
        integrity_log("pager", f"poke:{job}")
        self.send(cid, f"{job}: waking the vault. This takes a while.")
        # RELEASE IT IF THE THREAD NEVER STARTS. The worker releases `busy` in
        # its own finally, so a Thread.start() that raises leaves the lock held
        # by nobody -- and every later poke answers "a wake is already running"
        # for the life of the process. Reproduced with a Thread whose start()
        # raises RuntimeError("can't start new thread"), which is exactly what
        # a 1 GB Pi under memory pressure does.
        try:
            threading.Thread(target=self._worker, args=(cid, job, params),
                             daemon=True).start()
        except BaseException as e:                           # noqa: BLE001
            self.busy.release()
            integrity_log("pager", "worker_start_failed")
            self.send(cid, f"{job}: could not start the wake — {_redact(e)[:120]}")

    def run(self) -> int:
        print(f"  [+] Pager up. {len(self.allow)} allowlisted chat(s), "
              f"Tor only, one wake at a time.")
        while not shutdown_requested():
            for upd in self.updates():
                uid = upd.get("update_id")
                if isinstance(uid, int):
                    # Advance the cursor BEFORE acting: a message that crashes
                    # the handler must not be replayed on every restart.
                    self.limits.offset = max(self.limits.offset, uid + 1)
                    self.limits.save()
                try:
                    self.handle(upd)
                except Exception as e:                       # noqa: BLE001
                    print(f"  [!] update dropped: {_redact(e)[:200]}")
        print("  [*] Shutdown requested.")
        return 0


def whoami(token: str, proxies: dict, waits: int = 30) -> int:
    """Print the chat id of the next message, then stop. Arms nothing.

    Long-polls the same way the pager does, over the same Tor proxy, and
    reports the id and nothing that could be mistaken for a command having
    been run.
    """
    print("  [*] Waiting for a message. Open Telegram, find your bot, and "
          "send it anything.")
    print("      Ctrl-C to stop. This does not wake anything.")
    seen = set()
    for _ in range(waits):
        if shutdown_requested():
            break
        url = (f"https://api.telegram.org/bot{token}/getUpdates"
               f"?timeout={POLL_HOLD_S}&allowed_updates=%5B%22message%22%5D")
        try:
            d = safe_get(url, proxies=proxies)
        except Exception as e:                               # noqa: BLE001
            print(f"  [!] poll failed: {_redact(e)}")
            time.sleep(5)
            continue
        if not isinstance(d, dict) or not isinstance(d.get("result"), list):
            time.sleep(2)
            continue
        for upd in d["result"]:
            msg = (upd or {}).get("message") or {}
            cid = (msg.get("chat") or {}).get("id")
            if not isinstance(cid, int) or cid in seen:
                continue
            seen.add(cid)
            # Every one of these is a string the sender chose, and this line
            # goes to a terminal and, under systemd, a journal. _no_ctrl as
            # well as terminal_safe: see _no_ctrl for what the second one does
            # that the first does not.
            who = _no_ctrl(terminal_safe(
                str((msg.get("from") or {}).get("username")
                    or (msg.get("chat") or {}).get("title") or "?")))[:40]
            print(f"\n  [+] chat id: {cid}   (from: {who})")
            print(f"      Start the pager with --chat-id {cid}")
            print(f"      A chat id is NOT a secret -- it is an allowlist "
                  f"entry, so it is fine on a command line. The token is not.")
        if seen:
            return 0
    print("  [!] No message arrived. The bot token may belong to a different "
          "bot than the one you are messaging.")
    return 1


def build_cli():
    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser(
        description="GhostSpiral Telegram pager (Pi side). Triggers a wake; "
                    "never carries an address, a memo or an amount.")
    # NOT required=True, because --whoami exists to be run BEFORE you have
    # either of them. Enforced after parsing instead, where the rule can say
    # which mode it is talking about.
    ap.add_argument("--key", default="",
                    help="this Pi's keyfile, as gs_doorbell wake takes it")
    ap.add_argument("--chat-id", action="append", default=None, type=int,
                    help="allowlisted Telegram chat id. Repeatable. Anything "
                         "from another chat is ignored without a reply.")
    ap.add_argument("--whoami", action="store_true",
                    help="print the chat id of the next message this bot "
                         "receives, then exit. Arms nothing, wakes nothing, "
                         "needs no --key and no --chat-id. This is how you "
                         "find the number --chat-id wants.")
    ap.add_argument("--token-file", default="",
                    help=f"0400 file holding the bot token. Or set "
                         f"{TOKEN_ENV} in this process's environment. NEVER "
                         f"on the command line.")
    ap.add_argument("--tor-proxy", default="socks5h://127.0.0.1:9050",
                    help="this Pi's own Tor. OPSEC_SETUP.md section 4: the "
                         "bot talks to Telegram ONLY through this, and does "
                         "not start if it is down.")
    ap.add_argument("--state", default="/var/lib/ghostspiral/pager_state.json",
                    help="0600 file holding the update cursor and rate "
                         "counters, so a restart is not a bypass")
    ap.add_argument("--min-interval", type=int,
                    default=DEFAULT_MIN_INTERVAL_S,
                    help="courtesy seconds between pokes")
    ap.add_argument("--daily-cap", type=int, default=DEFAULT_DAILY_CAP,
                    help="courtesy pokes per 24h. The vault's own keyfile "
                         "budget is the real bound.")
    ap.add_argument("--no-jitter", action="store_true",
                    help="passed through to the wake: skip the pre-WOL delay")
    return ap


def main(argv=None) -> int:
    install_signal_handlers()
    args = build_cli().parse_args(argv)
    try:
        proto.require_nacl()
    except proto.WakeError as e:
        sys.exit(f"[!] {e}")

    # FAIL CLOSED ON TOR, BEFORE ANYTHING ELSE. OPSEC_SETUP.md section 4: "If
    # Tor is down, the bot does not start."
    proxy = validate_proxy(args.tor_proxy)
    verify_tor(proxy)
    proxies = isolated_proxy(args.tor_proxy, "pager")

    token = load_token(args.token_file)

    # THE BOOTSTRAP, AND THE REASON IT HAD TO EXIST.
    #
    # --chat-id wants a number the operator has no way to know. The bot ignores
    # every chat that is not already on the allowlist -- deliberately, in
    # silence, because a reply confirms the bot is alive to whoever found it --
    # so pressing Start in Telegram produces nothing at all, and there is no
    # path from "I have a bot token" to "I have my chat id". The tool was
    # unusable from a standing start and only somebody who already knew to go
    # read getUpdates by hand could get past it.
    #
    # This arms nothing: no keyfile is read, no doorbell is built, no magic
    # packet can be sent from this branch. It prints a number and exits.
    if args.whoami:
        return whoami(token, proxies)

    if not args.key or not args.chat_id:
        sys.exit("[!] --key and --chat-id are both required to run the pager.\n"
                 "    Run with --whoami first (it needs neither) to find the "
                 "chat id.")
    key = doorbell().load_key(Path(args.key))

    st = Path(args.state)
    try:
        st.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    except OSError as e:
        sys.exit(f"[!] cannot create {st.parent}: {e.strerror}")

    integrity_log("pager", "start")
    return Pager(args, token, key, proxies).run()


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
